{"id":"0449c953-ccfa-4f2c-a330-e490b9f18e6f","object":"chat.completion","created":1784992649,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    def get_subtree(nid):\\n        subtree = {nid}\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            for child in children.get(cur, []):\\n                subtree.add(child)\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return subtree\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    order = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    for nid in order:\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if nid == root_id or not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        subtree = get_subtree(nid)\\n        retained_outside = retained - subtree\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(node_map[n]['symbol'] == sym for n in retained_outside):\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            retained -= subtree\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python solve(case) function for SE-PE-01. The task is about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The payload contains nodes (list of dicts) with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility, and required_symbols. The objective: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and not removing root or nodes with retained children. Deeper nodes first; use node ID as tie-break. Return removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted? likely sorted). Need to implement a greedy reduction: iterate nodes from deepest to shallowest (by depth descending, tie by ID ascending?), decide whether to remove. Must ensure that after removal, all required symbols remain present (by checking the retained tree's symbols). Also cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., if any descendant retained). However the description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we need to determine which nodes are retained after the process. The process: tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. That suggests we simulate removal and check if required symbols are still present in the nodes that would remain (including ancestors, but note that removal of a node also removes its descendants? The tree is hierarchical: if you remove a node, all its descendants are also removed? The problem statement: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Typically in tree reduction, removing a node removes its entire subtree. But careful: \"node with retained children\" - if a node has children that are retained, cannot remove it. That implies removing a node would also remove its children (since children depend on parent). So the removal of a node implies its subtree is gone. So we need to consider that when checking required symbols. So algorithm: start with all nodes as candidates for removal (except root and non-removable). Process in order: deepest first (descending depth), for equal depth, use ID as tie-breaker (ascending? Usually stable tie break using ID as the order, likely ascending ID). For each node: if it is removable and not root and not already removed? Actually we'll track retained set. Initially all nodes are retained. For each node in order, check if removing it (and its subtree) would still satisfy required_symbols. That is, compute the set of symbols that would remain if we remove that node and all its descendants. The remaining nodes are all nodes except the subtree rooted at this node. We need to ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. Also we must ensure that no retained children? Actually the condition \"node with retained children\" means if the node has any children that are currently retained (i.e., not removed yet), then we cannot remove it because those children would be removed. In our order, we are processing deepest first, so when we consider a node, its children might have already been considered for removal. If a child was removed, then it's not a retained child. So the condition is: node can be removed only if all its children are also removed (i.e., none of its direct children are currently retained). Because if any child is retained, removing parent would also remove that child, violating the condition. So we must check that the node's children are not in the retained set. However careful: The condition might also extend to deeper descendants? The phrasing \"retained children\" likely means immediate children that are retained. But if a descendant is retained, the parent will also have that descendant as a descendant, so the parent cannot be removed because that would remove the descendant. So the check is that the entire subtree rooted at the node must be considered for removal. The algorithm: we process nodes in post-order (deepest first). For a node, if it is removable (and not root), we check if removing it (i.e., discarding its entire subtree) still leaves all required symbols represented among the nodes that are not in its subtree (including ancestors and other branches). Also, we need to ensure that the node does not have any retained children? Actually if we remove the node, all its descendants are also removed, regardless of whether they were retained before. So if any descendant is required (i.e., its symbol is required and there's no other node with that symbol outside the subtree), we cannot remove it. So that's covered by the required symbols check. The condition \"node with retained children\" is essentially the same as checking that the subtree is not needed for required symbols. However the phrase might be an additional constraint: you cannot remove a node if it has any retained child, even if the required symbols are met elsewhere. That seems odd because if the child is retained, it means we intend to keep it, so removing the parent would also remove the child, contradicting retention. So indeed, we should only consider nodes for removal if they are leaf nodes or if all their descendants have already been removed (i.e., none of their children are retained). In our processing order from deepest to shallowest, when we consider a node, its descendants have already been processed (so either removed or kept). If any descendant was kept, then this node cannot be removed because removing it would remove those descendants. So we need to check that all descendants are not retained (i.e., already removed). That is equivalent to checking that the node has no retained children (since if any descendant is retained, the immediate child on that path is retained). So we can check that for each direct child, it is not in the retained set. This ensures no retained descendants.\n\nBut what about the required symbols? Even if no retained descendants, we still need to check that the node's symbol is not the only occurrence of a required symbol. Also root cannot be removed. And nodes with removable=false cannot be removed.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build mapping node_id -> node dict.\n- Build parent mapping.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Initially retained = set of all node ids.\n- Determine order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id ascending (ID is string, typical lexicographic order, but could be numeric; use natural sort? The spec says \"node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\". No indication of type. In examples, IDs are like \"n1\", \"n2\". So we can sort by depth descending, then by id using string comparison (Python default). That should be fine.\n- removed_nodes = list.\n- For each node in order:\n  - if node_id == root: skip (root cannot be removed).\n  - if not node['removable']: skip.\n  - Check that for all direct children (nodes with parent == node_id), none are in retained set. If any child retained, skip this node (cannot remove because it would delete retained children).\n  - Compute the required symbols after removal. The remaining nodes are retained minus this subtree. Subtree includes node and all descendants. We can compute descendant set. But easier: we can check if the node's symbol is required and if there is any other node (outside the subtree) with that symbol. Also check for other required symbols that might be only in the subtree.\n  - Approach: For each required_symbol, count how many nodes with that symbol are currently retained. If we remove the subtree, we lose the nodes in subtree that have that symbol. So we need that for each required symbol, the count of nodes with that symbol in the retained set minus the count in the subtree is >= 1.\n  - We need to know the counts per symbol in the retained set and in the subtree. Since the retained set changes as we remove nodes, we need to maintain counts dynamically. We can maintain a dictionary symbol -> set of node ids that have that symbol among retained nodes. Initially, populate for all nodes. When we remove a node, we remove its id from the sets for its symbol and all symbols of its descendants. But easier: we can just check on the fly using the current retained set and compute subtree symbol counts by traversing subtree. Since the input size is small (examples have 7 nodes), we can do it for each node. O(n^2) fine.\n  - So for node in order, compute subtree_ids = set of node_id and all descendant ids (by traversing from node down using parent mapping). Then compute retained_outside = retained - subtree_ids. Then for each req_sym in required_symbols, check if any node in retained_outside has that symbol. If all required symbols have at least one occurrence in retained_outside, then removal is valid.\n  - If valid, then:\n    - remove node_id from retained set.\n    - Also remove all descendant ids from retained set (since they are part of subtree). Actually if we remove the node, the entire subtree is gone. So we need to remove all subtree_ids from retained.\n    - Append node_id to removed_nodes list.\n    - Also, we need to update something? For subsequent nodes, the retained set is now smaller, and the subtree of a parent will no longer include these removed nodes, so they won't block the parent's removal. The check for retained children will work because the child's id is no longer in retained. So fine.\n  - Note: After removal, we should also update symbol counts, but we recompute each time, so no need to maintain counts.\n\n- At the end, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained))? The output schema says arrays of strings, order? For removed_node_ids, it's the order in which they are removed (the algorithm). The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (ascending). removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the order we appended). That matches.\n\nEdge cases: root is non-removable, symbol \"root\" is required? required_symbols includes \"root\". So root must stay. Also other required symbols like \"s1\". Ensure that after reduction, root and at least one node with s1 remain.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: case is a dict with keys \"nodes\", \"required_symbols\", maybe \"_case_nonce\" etc. Use case directly.\n\n2. Build dictionaries:\n   - node_map = {node['id']: node}\n   - children_map = {} id -> list of child ids\n   - root_id = None\n   - for node in nodes:\n       if node['parent'] is None: root_id = node['id']\n       else: children_map.setdefault(node['parent'], []).append(node['id'])\n\n3. Build depth mapping? Already have depth in node.\n\n4. Define function get_subtree_ids(node_id):\n   Use BFS/DFS to collect all descendants including node_id.\n\n5. required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n\n6. retained = set of all node ids.\n\n7. removed_node_ids = []\n\n8. Order list of node ids: sorted by (-node['depth'], node['id'])\n\n9. For each node_id in order:\n   - node = node_map[node_id]\n   - if node_id == root_id: continue\n   - if not node['removable']: continue\n   - # Check retained children: if any child in children_map[node_id] is in retained, skip.\n   - children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\n   - if any(child in retained for child in children): continue\n   - # Compute subtree\n   - subtree = get_subtree_ids(node_id)\n   - # Check required symbols\n   - retained_outside = retained - subtree\n   - ok = all(\n         any(node_map[nid]['symbol'] == req_sym for nid in retained_outside)\n         for req_sym in required_symbols\n     )\n   - if ok:\n       # Remove entire subtree\n       retained -= subtree\n       removed_node_ids.append(node_id)\n\n10. After loop:\n   - retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)  # sorted ascending by string\n   - Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nTest with given fixture examples. We need to mentally simulate for a case.\n\nLet's take case 0:\nNodes: root(d0, removable false), n1(d1, parent root, removable false, symbol s1), n2(d1, removable true, symbol s2), n3(d1, removable false, symbol s0), n4(d2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1), n5(d2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2), n6(d2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0). Required: root, s1.\n\nProcessing order: depth 2 first: n4, n5, n6 (order by id: n4, n5, n6? id string: \"n4\"<\"n5\"<\"n6\"? lexicographic: n4, n5, n6). Then depth 1: n1, n2, n3 (id order: n1,n2,n3). Then depth 0: root (skip).\n\n- n4: removable true, parent n2. Children: none. retained_outside = all nodes except {n4}. required symbols: root (n1? root has symbol root, n1 has symbol s1, n3 has s0, n5 has s2, n6 has s0, n2 has s2, n4 has s1). After removing n4, we still have n1 with s1, root with root. So ok. Remove n4 and its subtree (only n4). retained becomes all except n4. removed_node_ids = ['n4'].\n\n- n5: removable false => skip.\n\n- n6: removable true, parent n1, no children. retained_outside = all except {n6}. required: root and s1 - after removing n6, we still have root (root), s1 (n1). So ok. Remove n6. retained = all except n4,n6. removed = ['n4','n6'].\n\n- depth 1: n1: removable false => skip.\n- n2: removable true, parent root. Children: n4 is already removed (not in retained), so no retained children. Subtree of n2 includes n2 and n4 (but n4 already removed). Actually subtree computed includes n2 and all descendants in original tree, regardless of retained status. But when we compute retained_outside, we subtract the subtree set (including n2 and n4). Since n4 is already removed, it's not in retained anyway. The retained_outside = retained - {n2, n4, (any other descendants? n4 is only child)} = all nodes except n2, n4. Does this include n1 (with s1) and root? Yes. So required symbols satisfied? root present, s1 present (n1). So ok. Remove n2 and its subtree (n2,n4). But n4 already not in retained, so just remove n2. retained = all except n4,n6,n2. removed = ['n4','n6','n2'].\n\n- n3: removable false => skip.\n\nNow retained nodes: root, n1, n5. Symbols: root, s1 (n1), s2 (n5). Required root and s1 satisfied. retained_node_ids sorted: ['n1', 'n5', 'root']? Actually root is 'root', n1, n5. sorted string: 'n1' < 'n5' < 'root'. So returned: removed ['n4','n6','n2'], retained ['n1','n5','root'].\n\nCheck if that makes sense: The root stays, n1 is required (non-removable) with s1, n5 is non-removable depth2 under n3 (child of n3). That's fine.\n\nNow check other cases? Probably similar.\n\nWe need to ensure we handle cases where required symbols are already satisfied by other nodes after removal.\n\nEdge: If root is not the only node with symbol \"root\"? It is unique. So root cannot be removed.\n\nThe solution seems straightforward.\n\nNow need to output the implementation as JSON string under \"implementation\" key.\n\nWe must not use any external libraries, only standard library. Use only Python 3.\n\nWe need to escape the source code properly. Write the code as a string.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    # build subtree cache? can compute on the fly\n    def get_subtree(nid):\n        subtree = {nid}\n        stack = [nid]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            for child in children.get(cur, []):\n                subtree.add(child)\n                stack.append(child)\n        return subtree\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # sort by depth desc, id lexicographic asc\n    order = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    for nid in order:\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        if nid == root_id or not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        subtree = get_subtree(nid)\n        retained_outside = retained - subtree\n        # check required symbols\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            # check if any node in retained_outside has this symbol\n            if not any(node_map[n]['symbol'] == sym for n in retained_outside):\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            retained -= subtree\n            removed.append(nid)\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\n\nPotential issues: The subtree calculation includes nodes that may have already been removed from retained, but that's fine. The check for retained children uses children.get(nid, []), but it's using the original children mapping which includes all children even if they have been removed. That's correct: we want to check if any child is still retained. If child was removed, it's not in retained, so condition fails. So that works.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure we don't incorrectly skip a node because its child is still retained but that child will be removed later? Actually we process deeper first, so children are processed before parents. If a child is retained (not removed during its turn), then when we get to parent, the child is still retained, so we cannot remove the parent. That's correct because we should not remove a parent if it would remove a retained child. The child was kept for a reason (likely it's non-removable or its removal would violate required symbols). So that's intended.\n\nOne nuance: What if a child is kept because its symbol is required, but later the parent could be removed if the required symbol is also present elsewhere? But that would still remove the child, which is not allowed because the child is retained. So we must not remove parent if any child is retained. This aligns with \"node with retained children\".\n\nThus algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow write as required: response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a string. Must not use Markdown fences. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nWe'll output the code as a JSON string.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4556,"total_tokens":6725,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4163},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}